    ],
    "etymology": {
     "text": "From Middle High German ëȥȥen, Old High German ëȥȥan, from Proto-Germanic *etaną, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁ed-; cognate with English eat and Dutch eten.",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "Animals do not ‘essen’ in German, they ‘fressen’ — using fressen for a person is rude or jokey.",
     "The past participle is gegessen, with an extra -ge-; the old form gessen is obsolete."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "Stem vowel e → i: du isst, er isst (not *esst); and du isst / er isst are spelled the same.",
     "Past tense with ß: ich aß, wir aßen."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "Soup is eaten in German (Suppe essen), not drunk — unlike English ‘drink soup’ in some contexts or Chinese 喝汤."
    ]
